THE aarch64 MIRROR OF THE x86 HOLE: the 4-wide chroma CDEF arm carried ONE row
of four columns per 128-bit register, a quarter of the lanes doing useful work.
Moving it to TWO rows of four i16 columns — the same biased-u16 form the 8-wide
NEON arm has always used — makes the kernel **1.6x faster: 185-188 ns -> 113-119
ns with disjoint confidence intervals over three independent runs of each
build.** Byte-identical everywhere.

**AND THE WHOLE-FRAME WALL CLOCK IS A NULL, which is reported as one.** On the
only host that can run this code the frame A/B reads 0.9973 at p6 against a
SAME-BINARY control of 0.9965 — the change is indistinguishable from no change
at that resolution. The two statements are not in conflict and both are kept:
the kernel is measurably faster, and **no frame-level benefit is established.**

date:   2026-09-05
host:   THIS Mac (M4 Pro, aarch64) — the only host that runs the NEON arm.
        **There is NO Ir number in this record and none is claimed: callgrind
        is Linux-only.** r7900x cannot execute this code.
base:   `origin/main 84621b20` (the find_dir AVX2 commit), immediately prior.
cand:   this commit. `crates/svtav1-dsp/src/cdef.rs` +
        `crates/svtav1-dsp/benches/kernel_tiers.rs` (the new bench case).
data:   benchmarks/cdef_neon_4wide_i16_2026-09-05.tsv.

=================== WHAT CHANGED ===================

`cdef_load4x2_bias_neon` (new) loads 4 columns from each of TWO rows into one
biased `uint16x8_t` — lanes 0..3 row `i`, lanes 4..7 row `i + sub` — via
`vcombine_u16` of two `vld1_u16`, then the `^ 0x8000` bias
[`cdef_load8_bias_neon`] already documents. `cdef_filter_cols4_neon` then runs
the SAME `cdef_constrain8_bias_neon` / `vmulq_s16` / `vminq_u16` /
`vbslq_u16` chain the 8-wide arm uses, over eight lanes instead of four i32
lanes, and steps `i += 2 * sub`. The i32 tail is unchanged and shared with the
8-wide arm, for the reason that arm's comment already gives.

The caller gains the same shape guard the x86 dispatcher got: if
`rows % (2 * sub) != 0` it falls back to `cdef_filter_block_core` rather than
mis-striding. Every shape the encoder produces divides evenly (BLOCK_4X4 at
`sub_uv = min(cfg.subsampling, 1)` = 1, BLOCK_4X8 at 1 or 2).

Nothing about the 8-wide arm, the HBD arm, or the x86 arms is touched.

=================== THE MEASUREMENT, AND WHY IT IS A KERNEL BENCH ===================

**A whole-frame A/B cannot resolve this change on this box, and that was
measured rather than assumed.** `perf_ab.sh` with a SAME-BINARY control on this
Mac reads a floor of 0.9965 at p6 with a 0.9940-1.0021 span — a +-0.4 % noise
band, on a laptop that carries ambient load (a `qemu-system-x86_64`, the
WallpaperAerials extension, and this session's own processes; `uptime` 1.45-1.71
throughout, captured before and after). The 4-wide arm is roughly half the CDEF
filter's pixels at p6 and the CDEF filter is ~1.9 % of the frame after
`cdef_i16_kernel_2026-09-05`, so even a 40 % kernel saving is ~0.4 % of the
frame — at, not above, the floor.

So the honest instrument is the kernel, and the repo already has the harness for
it: `crates/svtav1-dsp/benches/kernel_tiers.rs` (zenbench — interleaved
round-robin, paired statistics). It had a `cdef_filter_block_8x8` case and no
4-wide one; **this commit adds `cdef_filter_block_4x4`**, which is the shape the
encoder hands the filter for 4:2:0 chroma and 10,752 of the 26,816 calls on
photo_cid 512² p6. Upgrading the existing bench rather than writing a one-off
script is the workspace's own rule.

`cdef_filter_block_4x4`, NEON arm, three independent runs of each build,
alternating base and cand:

    build   run 1              run 2              run 3
    base    188.5 [173-202]    185.0 [170-200]    187.0 [169-204]
    cand    112.9 [102-123]    118.0 [107-129]    119.0 [108-129]

**Every cand interval lies entirely below every base interval** (highest cand
upper bound 129 ns against the lowest base lower bound 169 ns). The ratio is
**1.58x - 1.67x**.

`cdef_filter_block_8x8`, NEON arm — a path this commit does NOT touch, kept as
an in-run control:

    base    495.5 [385-704]    351.0 [317-386]    375.0 [339-408]
    cand    373.2 [334-426]    346.0 [314-382]    345.0 [318-368]

Runs 2 and 3 overlap on both sides; **the first base run's 495 ns with its very
wide 385-704 interval is an outlier**, and it is recorded rather than dropped
because it is the reason three runs were taken instead of one. Read as a null,
which is what an untouched path should read.

WHOLE FRAME, `perf_ab.sh`, photo_cid 512x512 qp 40, 31 interleaved randomised
paired rounds, one untimed warmup per sample, neither side niced, every row
ident=Y:

    pair      preset  ratio   speedup  p25      p75
    CONTROL   2       1.0003  1.000    0.9967   1.0032
    CONTROL   6       0.9965  1.004    0.9940   1.0021
    AB        2       0.9992  1.001    0.9956   1.0009
    AB        6       0.9973  1.003    0.9931   1.0015

Corrected for their own controls the two cells read 0.9989 and 1.0008 — i.e.
**nothing, in both directions.** The A/B spans sit on top of the control spans.
**This is a NULL and is reported as one.**

=================== WHY IT IS KEPT RATHER THAN REVERTED ===================

Stated plainly because the campaign's standing rule is "report nulls and revert
their code":

* The rule exists to stop code being kept on a hope. Here the kernel effect is
  MEASURED and well separated (six runs, disjoint intervals, 1.6x), on the
  correct instrument for a change of this size.
* The frame-level A/B is not evidence of no effect; it is evidence that this
  box cannot resolve an effect of the expected size. Its own same-binary
  control says so.
* The change is byte-identical (gates below) and reduces code diversity rather
  than adding it — the 4-wide arm now runs the same biased-u16 chain as the
  8-wide arm instead of a second i32 formulation with its own `constrain`
  helper.

**What is NOT claimed: any frame-level or wall-clock gain.** If a later session
wants that number it needs either a quiet aarch64 box or a cycle counter, not a
longer `perf_ab` run on a laptop.

=================== EXACTNESS ===================

The two `cdef` exactness tests and the eleven `tests/c_parity_cdef.rs` tests
cover this arm unchanged — `cdef_filter_block_simd_matches_scalar_over_the_legal_knob_domain`
sweeps all four `bsize`s (BLOCK_4X4 and BLOCK_4X8 among them) x 8 dirs x 16
primary x 4 secondary x 5 dampings x 3 subsampling factors on nine pixel
patterns, through the public `incant!` dispatcher inside
`for_each_token_permutation` with the report consumed.

POSITIVE CONTROL, run deliberately: offsetting the second row's base index by
one (`b1 = .. + 1`) in the new loader makes the unit test AND three
`c_parity_cdef` tests FAIL; reverting restores them.

